    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new `Salutation` class slots cleanly into the established pattern for this mapper:\n\n- **Right unit, right package.** `OwnerMapper` already externalizes any derived field that involves a branch/condition into a small `final` helper with a private constructor and a static `of(Owner)` \u2014 exactly what `ContactPreference` and `AgeBand` do, both in the `mapper` package. Trivial one-liners (`displayName`, `initials`) stay inline; `salutation` has a title-present/absent branch, so giving it a class is consistent. Placing it in `mapper` rather than `model` is also the correct call: `OwnerIdentity`'s own javadoc spells out the layering rule \u2014 a derivation lives in `model` only because it has *multiple* consumers (mapper + create pipeline), whereas a response-only derivation with a single consumer (the mapper) belongs beside the mapper. `salutation` is a read-only response field consumed only here, so `mapper` is the natural home alongside `ContactPreference`/`AgeBand`.\n\n- **No duplication, not a catch-all.** The logic isn't repeated anywhere, and nothing existing was bloated \u2014 `OwnerMapper` gains one expression line, `Owner` gains a standard field + getter/setter mirroring its siblings.\n\n- **Idiomatic details match.** Javadoc structure/wording mirrors `ContactPreference`/`AgeBand` precisely; `of(Owner)` naming matches the sibling helpers; the null/blank title guard is reasonable and documented. The schema additions (all four dialects) and the OpenAPI `title`/`salutation` entries follow the surrounding field conventions.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this entirely natural \u2014 no structural changes needed.",
